Eval ctors in a module whose memory cannot be flattened.

`wasm-ctor-eval` gives up on the whole module unless `MemoryUtils::flatten`
succeeds, and its own comment calls that a TODO. Flattening fails on a passive
data segment, and on any expression that *names* a segment -- `array.new_data`
is one, and it is how a GC language materialises a constant array or a string
literal. So for a WasmGC program the tool evaluates nothing at all, however
little the ctors touch linear memory.

Flattening buys exactly one thing, and it is not interpretation. Reading memory
does not need it: the interpreter seeds each active segment at its own constant
offset, and the external interface's memory is a vector grown from address
zero. `array.new_data` does not need it either; it reads its segment directly,
and unflattened the segment's identity is still intact. What needs it is
`applyMemoryToModule`, which dumps the final image into `dataSegments[0]` and
asserts that its offset is zero.

So: remember whether flattening succeeded, and if it did not, refuse the writes
instead of the module. A store after instantiation throws `FailToEvalException`,
which fails that one ctor and lets the others run; and the memory is not written
back, there being nothing in it that execution put there.

`data.drop` is the exception. It is the one memory effect that never reaches the
external interface -- the interpreter records it in a set of its own -- so it
cannot be caught one ctor at a time, and a module containing one is still given
up on.

--- a/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp
+++ b/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp
@@ -301,6 +301,11 @@
 // Whether to emit informative logging to stdout about the eval process.
 static bool quiet = false;
 
+// Whether linear memory was flattened into the one segment at offset 0 that
+// applyMemoryToModule() writes an evalled memory image back into. Set by
+// canEval(), which is where the reasoning is.
+static bool flattenedMemory = true;
+
 struct CtorEvalExternalInterface : EvallingModuleRunner::ExternalInterface {
   Module* wasm;
   EvallingModuleRunner* instance;
@@ -342,7 +347,9 @@
     }
 
     // If nothing was ever written to memories then there is nothing to update.
-    if (!memories.empty()) {
+    // An unflattened memory cannot be written back at all, and nothing was
+    // written to it either: doStore() fails the ctor rather than let it.
+    if (flattenedMemory && !memories.empty()) {
       applyMemoryToModule();
     }
 
@@ -533,6 +540,13 @@
   }
 
   template<typename T> void doStore(Address address, T value, Name memoryName) {
+    if (instanceInitialized && !flattenedMemory) {
+      // Nothing would carry this into the module: applyMemoryToModule() needs
+      // a single segment at offset 0. Fail this ctor, and only this one.
+      // Seeding the active segments at instantiation arrives here too, and
+      // must not be refused -- hence instanceInitialized.
+      throw FailToEvalException("write to unflattened memory");
+    }
     Bits::writeLE<T>(value, getMemory(address, memoryName, sizeof(T)));
   }
 
@@ -1522,13 +1536,30 @@
